# Target C-II — adjacent switching diameter directions lie in one open semicircle

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only exact structural strengthening
**Scope:** any adjacent O--O strip in the audited switching-order theorem
**Dependencies:** adjacent switching one-arc order; convexity of the source hull; uniqueness of a planar diameter pair in one projective direction; three-ray strip blocker theorem
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The adjacent-strip blocker theorem leaves a generic Caratheodory alternative in which
three switching diameter directions alone positively surround the origin, and an exact
KKT alternative in which the switching diameter resultant vanishes. Both alternatives
are incompatible with the boundary location of the switching diameter endpoints.

For a switching actual diameter edge `h=xm`, orient it from the endpoint outside the
middle strip layer into its middle-layer endpoint and write

\[
 m-x=Dd_h,
 \qquad |d_h|=1.
 \tag{OS.1}
\]

Then the complete switching direction set satisfies

\[
\boxed{\{d_h:h\in H_{sw}\}\text{ is contained in one open semicircle of }S^1.}
\tag{OS.2}
\]

Consequently:

1. no positive combination of the switching directions can vanish;
2. the exact strip KKT resultant
   \[
   Aa+Bb=D\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_hd_h
   \]
   is never zero;
3. in the non-antipodal boundary-contact case `a+b!=0`, every planar blocker
   certificate can be chosen to contain at least one boundary-contact ray and therefore
   at most **two switching diameter edges**;
4. the diameter-only three-ray blocker pattern is impossible;
5. in the antipodal case `b=-a`, the exact strip resultant forces `A!=B`.

Thus the current local blocker list shrinks from a possible three-diameter positive
circuit to one- or two-diameter patterns involving the boundary contacts.

---

## 1. A diameter direction is a support normal at both endpoints

Let `K=conv(A)` be the source convex hull and let `xm` be an actual diameter pair.
Orient

\[
d={m-x\over D}.
\]

For every source point `z`,

\[
d\cdot(z-x)\le |z-x|\le D=d\cdot(m-x).
\]

Hence

\[
d\cdot z\le d\cdot m.
\]

By convexity the same inequality holds for every `z in K`, so `d` belongs to the
outward normal cone of `K` at `m`.

Similarly,

\[
(-d)\cdot(z-m)\le |z-m|\le D=(-d)\cdot(x-m),
\]

and therefore `-d` belongs to the outward normal cone at `x`.

Thus every switching diameter contributes one support-normal direction at its middle
endpoint and the antipodal support-normal direction at its exterior endpoint:

\[
\boxed{
d_h\in N_K(m_h)\cap S^1,
\qquad
-d_h\in N_K(x_h)\cap S^1.
}
\tag{OS.3}

This is exact and does not use the Type-II near-disk limit.

---

## 2. Normal directions along a convex boundary arc form one circular interval

Traverse the boundary of a planar compact convex polygon counterclockwise. The outward
normal direction is monotone in circular angle: along an edge it is constant, and at a
vertex its normal cone fills the circular interval between the adjacent edge normals.

Therefore, for any connected boundary arc `I`, the union

\[
\mathcal N(I)
:=\bigcup_{p\in I} (N_K(p)\cap S^1)
\]

is one closed circular interval after including the endpoint one-sided normal cones.
For complementary boundary arcs `I,J`, choose the common endpoint conventions so that
their normal-angle lengths satisfy

\[
\operatorname{len}\mathcal N(I)
+
\operatorname{len}\mathcal N(J)
=2\pi.
\tag{OS.4}
\]

The switching diameter endpoints lie strictly inside the four open boundary blocks of
the adjacent-strip theorem, so no switching direction depends on how the two common arc
endpoints are assigned.

In particular, at least one of the two complementary normal sweeps has angular length
at most `pi`.

---

## 3. Both switch orientations: use the common middle arc or its complement

Assume both left-switch and right-switch rows occur. The audited one-arc switching
order theorem gives one connected middle boundary arc `I=B_M^star` containing **every**
switching middle endpoint. Every switching exterior endpoint belongs to the connected
complementary boundary arc

\[
J=\partial K\setminus I,
\]

possibly with unused middle-boundary points between the left and right exterior
blocks.

If

\[
\operatorname{len}\mathcal N(I)\le\pi,
\]

then (OS.3) gives

\[
d_h\in\mathcal N(I)
\quad\text{for every switching }h,
\]

so all `d_h` lie in one closed semicircle.

If instead

\[
\operatorname{len}\mathcal N(I)>\pi,
\]

then (OS.4) gives

\[
\operatorname{len}\mathcal N(J)<\pi.
\]

Equation (OS.3) gives

\[
-d_h\in\mathcal N(J)
\quad\text{for every switching }h,
\]

so all `d_h` lie in the antipodal open semicircle.

Thus in the mixed-orientation case the switching directions are contained in a closed
semicircle, with an open semicircle unless the relevant normal sweep has exact length
`pi`.

---

## 4. One switch orientation: use the exterior arc or its complement

Suppose only left-switch rows occur. Then every exterior switching endpoint lies on the
single connected block `E=B_L`. Middle endpoints may occupy either middle boundary
arc, but all of them lie in the complementary connected boundary arc

\[
J=\partial K\setminus E.
\]

If

\[
\operatorname{len}\mathcal N(E)\le\pi,
\]

then all `-d_h` lie in `\mathcal N(E)` and hence all `d_h` lie in one closed semicircle.
If its length exceeds `pi`, the complementary normal sweep has length below `pi`, and
all `d_h` lie in that open semicircle.

The all-right-switch case is identical with `E=B_R`.

Therefore every adjacent strip, with one or both switch orientations, satisfies a
closed-semicircle containment for the full switching direction set.

---

## 5. Parallel-diameter uniqueness makes the semicircle open

It remains only to exclude the possibility that the finite direction set occupies both
endpoints of a minimal closed semicircle of angular width exactly `pi`.

If that happened, two switching directions would be antipodal:

\[
d_{h_2}=-d_{h_1}.
\]

The corresponding actual diameter segments would be parallel in the same projective
direction. They are distinct graph edges: each switching edge appears once, with the
unique orientation from its exterior endpoint into its middle endpoint. The audited
parallel-diameter uniqueness lemma says a finite Euclidean set cannot contain two
distinct parallel diameter pairs.

Therefore no two switching directions are antipodal. A finite subset of a closed
semicircle with no antipodal pair is contained in a strictly shorter circular interval,
hence in an open semicircle. This proves (OS.2).

---

## 6. The zero strip-resultant branch is impossible

Use the relative-interior KKT stress from the strip blocker theorem, so every actual
diameter multiplier is strictly positive. The exact strip balance is

\[
Aa+Bb
=D\sum_{h\in H_{sw}}\beta_hd_h.
\tag{OS.5}
\]

Because the `d_h` lie in one open semicircle, there is a vector `w` with

\[
w\cdot d_h>0
\quad\text{for every switching }h.
\]

Hence

\[
w\cdot\sum_h\beta_hd_h>0,
\]

and the switching resultant cannot vanish. Therefore

\[
\boxed{Aa+Bb\ne0.}
\tag{OS.6}
\]

The zero-resultant three-direction Caratheodory branch of the current theorem is empty.
In particular, if `b=-a`, then

\[
(A-B)a\ne0,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{A\ne B.}
\tag{OS.7}
\]

---

## 7. Generic strip blockers use at most two diameter edges

Assume `a+b!=0`. The current three-ray theorem proves

\[
0\in\operatorname{conv}
\left(\{a,b\}\cup\{-d_h:h\in H_{sw}\}\right)
\tag{OS.8}
\]

and planar Caratheodory supplies a witness with at most three rays.

A witness using only switching-diameter rays would put

\[
0\in\operatorname{conv}\{-d_{h_1},-d_{h_2},-d_{h_3}\},
\]

contradicting the open-semicircle containment. Therefore every minimal witness contains
at least one of the two contact rays `a,b`. It consequently contains at most two
switching-diameter rays.

Thus the generic blocker types reduce to

```text
one contact + one diameter ray,
both contacts + one diameter ray,
one contact + two diameter rays,
```

with lower-cardinality degeneracies allowed. The diameter-only three-ray case is
impossible.

---

## 8. Interaction with the current 920-row/caterpillar refinement

The semicircle theorem is independent of the numerical switching-edge count. It applies
to the current main bound and to the stronger proof-only `<=920` Pareto refinement on
`research/c2-cusp-920-sol-20260915`.

The combined normal form is therefore

```text
finite adjacent switching layer cut
+ ordered one-arc caterpillar interface
+ all switching diameter directions in one open semicircle
+ nonzero exact switching KKT resultant
+ generic local blocker uses at most two switching diameter edges
+ antipodal boundary case retains the audited opposite-transverse two-edge obstruction.
```

No C-II closure is claimed. The next finite task is to classify the remaining one- and
two-diameter blocker patterns against the prefix/hub/suffix cyclic order and the two
Section-157 cut-resultant equations.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact support-normal strengthening of the adjacent
strip blocker classification. C-II and Level I remain open.
